    think:{quote:"Standing at last on the Radley porch beside the man she once thought a monster, Scout looks out at her own street and, for the length of one long moment, sees it exactly as Boo Radley must have seen it for all these years.", question:"This final image directly recalls Atticus's advice from the very first unit about climbing into someone else's skin. Why is it significant that Scout only achieves this fully with Boo Radley, and only at the very end of the novel?", options:["Because Boo Radley is the only character in the novel who matters.","Because it closes the novel's central moral arc, showing that Scout has finally learned, through experience rather than instruction, what empathy actually requires.","Because Scout no longer fears anyone in Maycomb after the trial."], answer:1, note:"Lee saves this culmination for the novel's final pages so that Scout's understanding arrives not as a rule she was told, but as a truth she has earned through fear, loss and the events of an entire year, giving the mockingbird image its fullest and most personal meaning."},
